{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"rebar's philosophy is three interfaces over shared functionality: CLI, MCP, and library. The bridge\nredesign must land on all three, not just the CLI.\n\nToday's MCP and library surfaces are shaped around the retiring vocabulary: `reconcile(mode=...)`\ntakes a mode STRING as user-supplied input (`_mcp_reads.py:291` gates mutating modes on\n`MODE_CAPS[parsed] != 0` behind `mcp.allow_jira_sync`), and `rebar.reconcile(mode=\"dry-run\")` is a\ndocumented library entry point with its own default. Retiring `--mode` changes the MCP tool's input\nschema and the library signature.\n\n`bridge_fsck()` is already exposed as both `rebar.bridge_fsck()` and an MCP tool with a published\n`outputSchema`; `bridge_status` is neither, so the redesign is free to choose its surface there.\n\nNote the library has its own divergent exit policy: `_lib_ops.py:115` raises unless the code is in\n`(0, 75)`, so a library caller hitting the normal in-flight case (3) gets a `RebarError` while CI\ntreats it as benign. **Ownership (review remediation): that policy belongs to sibling `9895`**, the\nexit-code ticket that moves the single 0/1/2 policy into core — this ticket CONSUMES the unified\npolicy rather than fixing it, so the two do not both edit `_lib_ops.py:115`. `f789` depends_on `9895`\nto enforce that order. Separately and IN SCOPE here: `_lib_ops.py:92` defaults `mode=\"dry-run\"`, a\nthird safety default that disappears with the mode vocabulary.\n\nSince `reconcile` retires with no alias, the MCP tool and library function retire with it — the\nsame reasoning applies: the defaults invert, so a silent rename would change behaviour.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every bridge verb with a machine-consumable result exists on CLI, MCP, and library\n- [ ] The MCP tools' input schemas carry no mode vocabulary\n- [ ] `rebar.reconcile()` (`src/rebar/__init__.py:51` -> `_lib_ops.py:92`) and the MCP `reconcile`\n      tool (`_mcp_reads.py:279`) are retired HERE, not in `4752` — `4752` retires the CLI + engine\n      only, so each surface's old verb is removed by the same change that ships its replacement and\n      no caller is left with neither. `f789` depends_on `4752`, which enforces that order\n- [ ] `_lib_ops.py` consumes the unified 0/1/2 exit policy landed by `9895`; this ticket does NOT\n      re-implement it, and a test asserts a library caller sees the same disposition as the CLI\n- [ ] MCP output schemas are published for the new verbs and covered by the existing schema-coverage test\n- [ ] `mcp.allow_jira_sync` still gates exactly the mutating surface"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786084706957577001,"uuid":"270e1f05-f67e-4626-9f45-a68fcdf95b48"}